WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.133
Syphilis is caused by Treponema pallidum.

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:05.460
In primary syphilis, focus on the local lesion: a

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:10.393
firm, indurated, painless chancre,
with firm, enlarged, nontender regional

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:10.900
lymph nodes.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:15.564
The supplied slides describe
microorganisms draining with lymphatic fluid.

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:20.937
Secondary syphilis has systemic
manifestations, including low-grade fever, malaise,

00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:25.580
sore throat, generalized lymph-node
enlargement, and skin or mucous-membrane

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:26.120
lesions.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.040
Watch the local site connect with the wider lymphatic

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:29.440
network.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:32.930
Latent syphilis can be
asymptomatic while medical evidence of

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:33.926
infection remains.

00:00:35.020 --> 00:00:37.393
Absence of symptoms does not mean the infection has

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:37.971
disappeared.

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:42.426
Tertiary disease can include
destructive gummas and neurosyphilis.

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.443
Keep the primary syphilitic
chancre separate from chancroid.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:50.400
The supplied chancroid slide
describes a painful, tender, soft

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:51.520
chancre.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.560
Use the lesion findings
and the disease stage together.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:58.020
This visual shows a
schematic sequence, not a prediction

00:00:58.020 --> 00:00:58.886
for every patient.
